#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:5
msgid "Extracting clean content and values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:7
msgid ""
"Sometimes what you want out of a page is the page itself, without "
"navigation, ads or footers, as the input of a search index, a summarizer or "
"a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline. See :ref:`clean-content`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:11
msgid ""
"Sometimes you know which parts of the page you want, you have already :ref:"
"`selected <topics-selectors>` them, and what you have is a string that is "
"not a value yet: a price with a currency symbol on it, a date written in "
"Spanish, a phone number written however its owner felt like. See :ref:"
"`parsing-values`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:20
msgid "Clean content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:22
msgid ""
"Trafilatura_ turns HTML into text or markdown, dropping boilerplate. Call it "
"on :attr:`response.text <scrapy.http.TextResponse.text>` from a callback:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:26
msgid ""
"import scrapy\n"
"import trafilatura\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"class ContentSpider(scrapy.Spider):\n"
"    name = \"content\"\n"
"    allowed_domains = [\"quotes.toscrape.com\"]\n"
"    start_urls = [\"https://quotes.toscrape.com/\"]\n"
"\n"
"    def parse(self, response):\n"
"        yield {\n"
"            \"url\": response.url,\n"
"            \"content\": trafilatura.extract(response.text, "
"output_format=\"markdown\"),\n"
"        }\n"
"        yield from response.follow_all(css=\"a\", callback=self.parse)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:44
msgid ""
"Save that as :file:`content.py` and write the whole crawl to a JSON Lines "
"file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:47
msgid "scrapy runspider content.py -O content.jsonl"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:51
msgid ""
"Title, author, date and site name come from a second call, which roughly "
"triples the time spent on each page, so ask for them only if you need them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:55
msgid ""
"metadata = trafilatura.extract_metadata(response.text)\n"
"yield {\n"
"    \"url\": response.url,\n"
"    \"title\": metadata.title,\n"
"    \"date\": metadata.date,\n"
"    \"content\": trafilatura.extract(response.text, "
"output_format=\"markdown\"),\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:66
msgid "Where the content is not prose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:68
msgid ""
"Trafilatura is tuned for articles, and it keeps what it is confident about. "
"On a product page or a listing, where most of the page is not prose, it "
"often returns a fraction of what you were after, or nothing at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:72
msgid ""
"There the answer is to :ref:`select <topics-selectors>` the parts you want. "
"For a part that is itself rich content, such as a product description or the "
"body of a post, clear-html_ normalizes the selected node into clean HTML or "
"text, keeping embedded images and videos:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:79
msgid ""
">>> from clear_html import clean_node, cleaned_node_to_html\n"
">>> from clear_html import cleaned_node_to_text\n"
">>> node = clean_node(response.css(\"article\")[0].root, response.url)\n"
">>> cleaned_node_to_html(node)\n"
"'<article>\\n\\n<p>Hi <strong>there</strong></p>\\n\\n</article>'\n"
">>> cleaned_node_to_text(node)\n"
"'Hi there'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:92
msgid "Pages you cannot download as they are"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:94
msgid ""
"If the content is not in the HTML that Scrapy downloads, it is loaded "
"dynamically; see :ref:`topics-dynamic-content`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:97
msgid ""
"If the website answers your requests with an error page or a challenge "
"instead of the content, see :ref:`bans`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:100
msgid ""
"`Zyte API`_ covers both, and through `scrapy-zyte-api`_ it can also return "
"the content already structured: its article extraction gives you the text of "
"an article and a simplified HTML version of its body, which markdownify_ "
"turns into markdown:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:107
msgid ""
">>> from markdownify import markdownify\n"
">>> article = response.raw_api_response[\"article\"]\n"
">>> markdownify(article[\"articleBodyHtml\"])\n"
"'Hi **there**'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:119
msgid "Parsing values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:121
msgid ""
"The following libraries turn a selected string into a value. They also work "
"as :ref:`input processors <topics-loaders-processors>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:126
msgid ""
"extruct_ reads JSON-LD, microdata, RDFa, Open Graph and Dublin Core out of a "
"page, which is often the cheapest source of a title, an author or a date:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:130
msgid ""
">>> import extruct\n"
">>> data = extruct.extract(response.text, base_url=response.url)\n"
">>> data[\"opengraph\"][0][\"properties\"]\n"
"[('og:title', 'Hi there')]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:137
msgid ""
"dateparser_ reads a date written in prose, in any of the languages it "
"supports:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:140
msgid ""
">>> import dateparser\n"
">>> dateparser.parse(\"12 de octubre de 2025\")\n"
"datetime.datetime(2025, 10, 12, 0, 0)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:146
msgid "`price-parser`_ separates the amount of a price from its currency:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:148
msgid ""
">>> from price_parser import Price\n"
">>> Price.fromstring(\"1.199,00 €\")\n"
"Price(amount=Decimal('1199.00'), currency='€')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:154
msgid "For numbers written out in words, use `number-parser`_ instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:156
msgid ""
"`zyte-parsers`_ parses fields out of a selected node: breadcrumbs, GTIN, "
"rating, review count, brand and, building on `price-parser`_, price."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:159
msgid ""
">>> from zyte_parsers import extract_breadcrumbs\n"
">>> extract_breadcrumbs(response.css(\"nav\")[0], base_url=response.url)\n"
"(Breadcrumb(name='Books', url='https://example.com/books'),)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:165
msgid ""
"phonenumbers_ turns a phone number as written into E.164, given the region "
"it belongs to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:168
msgid ""
">>> import phonenumbers\n"
">>> number = phonenumbers.parse(\"0664 123 4567\", \"AT\")\n"
">>> phonenumbers.format_number(number, phonenumbers.PhoneNumberFormat.E164)\n"
"'+436641234567'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:175
msgid "ftfy_ repairs text that reached you as mojibake:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:177
msgid ""
">>> import ftfy\n"
">>> ftfy.fix_text(\"The Mona Lisa doesnâ€™t have eyebrows.\")\n"
"\"The Mona Lisa doesn't have eyebrows.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:183
msgid "py3langid_ tells you which language a text is in:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:185
msgid ""
">>> import py3langid\n"
">>> py3langid.classify(\"Dieser Text ist auf Deutsch geschrieben.\")\n"
"('de', -154.48843383789062)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../topics/extraction.rst:193
msgid ""
"To parse a value out of JavaScript code in the page, see :ref:`topics-"
"parsing-javascript`."
msgstr ""
